# Paths whose CONTENT legitimately carries a credential shape, exempted one by one.
#
# Read by pablo-protocol's guards/credentials-guard.sh. A line is a path relative to this
# repository's root, matched exactly. Whole-line comments only.
#
# What this file does and does not do. The guard reads three content shapes — an issuer-prefixed
# key or token (`sk-ant-`, `ghp_`, a PEM header), a `NAME=value` assignment of a live-looking
# value, and a wrapped key beside its unwrapping parameters. Every path below matches one of them
# and holds no secret. The guard's own default is the strict one: these two policy files only ever
# RELAX the rule, so an absent or empty file exempts nothing. That is why each entry is a path and
# never a glob — `*.test.ts` would exempt every suite written from here on, including the one that
# accidentally pastes a real key, and the exemption would never announce itself.
#
# Each line below was read before it was added, and what makes it safe is stated. An entry whose
# justification cannot be written in one line is an entry that should not exist.

# One `docker run -e` sets the session-signing variable to a two-character throwaway for the
# container that serves a single smoke test, destroyed with the job. Every real secret in this file
# is a `${{ secrets.* }}` reference, which is a name and not a value.
.github/workflows/ci.yml

# The template for `.dev.vars`, which is gitignored. Every value is a placeholder in angle
# brackets or an issuer prefix with the body elided; the guard sees the assignment shape, not a
# value. This file is the one place that says which secrets exist, so dropping the shape is not an
# option — a template with no shape teaches nobody what to write.
apps/lexitar/.dev.vars.example

# Documents the same `.dev.vars` shape for a reader, with the value elided the same way.
apps/lexitar/AUTH.md

# Writes a local `.dev.vars` only when the developer has not already made one, from values that
# name themselves: each is the string `e2e-test-` followed by what it is for, and the one base64
# value decodes to a sentence saying it is a test key. Nothing here reaches a deployed environment.
apps/lexitar/scripts/e2e-serve.sh

# Seeds a loginable support agent into the LOCAL d1 only, defaulting its password to the word
# `support` beside a fixed account id. Both are reset on every boot; neither exists in prod.
apps/lexitar/scripts/provision-support-account.ts

# Fixture suites whose job is to contain the thing they pin, which is the one case where removing
# the shape removes the test. `dry-run-contract` sets the Anthropic key variable to a value whose
# own text says it is a unit-test value and not a real key; `server-error-middleware` asserts that a
# GitHub-token-shaped string — the alphabet, in order — is redacted out of an error report before it
# leaves the server.
#
# Which is the rule this file had to learn about itself: a justification quoting the shape it
# justifies carries that shape, and the guard refused this very file for naming one. The shapes are
# therefore described here and never spelled.
apps/lexitar/tests/unit/dry-run-contract.test.ts
apps/lexitar/tests/unit/server-error-middleware.test.ts
